course: dictionary
lesson: 7360
title: Words for places in towns
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A plaza is a word for a place in a town or city. Read the words around it. In «Maya cities like Tikal and Palenque had tall stone pyramids with temples on top, palaces and plazas», plazas comes in a list with pyramids, temples and palaces. These are all big, important parts of a city, so a plaza must be a place too.
explain: Use the swap check. Put your meaning in place of the hard word: "palaces and open squares" still makes sense, so you are on the right track. Now look at «In 2013 the Rana Plaza clothes-factory building collapsed in Bangladesh.» Here plaza is part of a name. The capital letters show that Rana Plaza is one particular building, not a square.
explain: Some words change meaning from place to place. In the USA, a plaza can be a shopping centre, as in «a shopping plaza». So when you read plaza, ask: is this a square outside, or a group of shops? The words around it will tell you which.
fact: A plaza is an open public square in a town or city, often paved, with benches and sometimes a fountain.
fact: In the USA and Canada, a plaza can also mean a shopping centre or a group of shops and offices.
fact: The word comes from Spanish, and plazas are often found in Spanish-speaking countries.
fact: Context check: in «palaces and plazas», the plazas are open spaces in a city where people can meet.
fact: Capital letters in names, like Rana Plaza, show one specific building or place.
exercise: «Maya cities had tall stone pyramids, temples, palaces and plazas.» What is a plaza? | A tall stone pyramid with a temple on top | A river that runs through a city | *An open public square in a town or city | A small room inside a palace
exercise: In the USA, «a shopping plaza» is what? | A school for young children | *A group of shops and offices | A fast road out of town | A quiet garden with a pond
exercise: How do you know «Rana Plaza» is the name of one building? | *The capital letters at the start of both words | It is the last word in the sentence | Plaza always means a square | The word clothes always means a plaza
test: Ella says a plaza is always a shop. Why is she wrong? | *Plazas can be open squares, and in the USA they can also mean shopping centres | A plaza is a type of fountain | Plazas are always inside buildings
test: «The plaza was full of people buying fruit and listening to music.» What is the plaza most likely to be? | A river crossing | A quiet room in a house | A narrow lane for cars only | *An open public square where crowds gather
exam: Which meaning of plaza is correct for «a shopping plaza»? | A paved square with benches and a fountain, in any country | A road that leads out of a city | A tall stone building with temples on top | *A group of shops and offices, especially in the USA
